       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still haven't entered the order. A confirmation alone isn't enough here, because there's no recognized clinical use for sertraline at 1000 mg a day.\n\n- The maximum labeled dose is 200 mg/day for all indications.\n- Even in specialist practice, doses above 200 mg are uncommon and rarely go much past 300 mg.\n- 1000 mg/day is in the range seen in overdoses. It carries a real risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures, QTc prolongation and arrhythmias.\n\nConfirming a 10\u00d7 error is a common way these mistakes get through, so I'd like to get this right before anything reaches the patient.\n\n**Here's what I can do:**\n1. **Enter a standard dose** (for example, 50 mg or 100 mg once daily). Tell me which and I'll enter it now.\n2. **Enter a higher-than-usual dose with a documented reason.** If there's a specific reason for going above 200 mg, such as psychiatry's recommendation or a known rapid-metabolizer status, tell me the dose and the reason. I'll enter it with that reason in the order note and flag it for pharmacist verification.\n3. **Talk to the pharmacist or the attending.** If 1000 mg came from another source, like a transcribed outside med list or a verbal order, please check it with them first.\n\nHow would you like to proceed?"
      }
     ],
